{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)-2g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3{a}_{5}-2{c}_{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315014 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic extensions of some functions from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $6\\text{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $5f(x)+2g(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c8b6a2b-4f05-511d-a20d-48b3e09d860a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315014 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x)∼\\frac{\\alpha {a}_{0}+\\beta {c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\alpha {a}_{n}+\\beta {c}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+(\\alpha {b}_{n}+\\beta {d}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $5f(x)+2g(x)$ are\n\n- $5{a}_{0}+2{c}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $5{a}_{n}+2{c}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $5{b}_{n}+2{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $6$th cosine coefficient of $5f(x)+2g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n5{a}_{6}+2{c}_{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315021 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $6$th cosine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbf29f66-74de-5a85-98a1-53dba6ce09ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315021 #edb/ref 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $6$th cosine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is ${a}_{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315022 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the Fourier constant coefficient of $f(-x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "295f6e2c-3159-5111-97f9-81196b1e35bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315022 #edb/ref 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the Fourier constant coefficient of $f(-x)$ is ${a}_{0}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315023 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-{x}^{2}$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71628329-43bb-5e3e-a2ae-9baba3c15766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315023 #edb/ref 1074 "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x)-(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315024 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+5$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a23b6b8d-69e5-5d60-9ec7-32ca455aba4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315024 #edb/ref 1074 "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x)+5 \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}+5 \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+5 \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397481 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8afd30f6-9418-5f9f-a8dd-bdb05d34a03e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397481 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397482 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397482 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397482 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397482 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397482 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186397484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397482 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "35a58589-56cd-5637-b523-03f859fac5b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397483 #edb/ref 1080 "even" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397483 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175